From u89c9fc822e34b78c at groups.google.com  Mon Oct 26 14:35:21 2015
From: u89c9fc822e34b78c at groups.google.com (Yves Rosseel)
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2015 14:35:21 +0000
Subject: Re: lavaan vs lm: standard errors
Message-ID: <T5_l6yYeaYA.Oh9eejHRBwAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <T5_l6yYeaYA.qWj1qzPGBwAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
References: <T5_l6yYeaYA.qWj1qzPGBwAJ@lavaan.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/lavaan/c/T5_l6yYeaYA/m/Oh9eejHRBwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

See this set of slides:

http://users.ugent.be/~yrosseel/lavaan/zurich2015/lavaan_zurich2015.pdf

in particular slide 42.

 From the lm fit, you can compute $\sigma^2$ as follows:

sum(resid(fit)^2) / nobs(fit)
# 2075.1

and you get the ML estimate reported by lavaan. If you didive by 'n - (p
+ 1)', you get

sum(resid(fit)^2) / (nobs(fit) - 5)
# 2184.316

and the sqrt of this number (46.73666) is wat lm's summary reports as
the residual standard error.

That is all there is to it: a different 'estimate' of $\sigma$, which is
used to scale the standard errors.

Yves.

